{
  "image_name": "0bf61db51142cec32397f0120adcd94f.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "The figure compares how epithelial cells are expelled from a tissue in normal versus mutant conditions. In the wild-type situation, extrusion is directed apically, meaning the cell is pushed upward into the lumen while the epithelial barrier stays intact. This is associated with basolateral S1P and its receptor S1P2, plus organized actin and microtubules that appear to coordinate contraction around the departing cell.\n\nIn contrast, APC-mutant cells extrude basally, downward toward the matrix and stroma. The diagram suggests that altered cytoskeletal organization and misplaced signaling components change the direction of force, so the cell leaves through the basal side rather than the apical side. A similar basal extrusion is shown for KRAS or S1P-mutant cells, but here the key feature is the apparent loss of S1P or S1P2 signaling, which again prevents normal apical expulsion.\n\nTaken together, the image highlights extrusion direction as a biologically important switch: normal epithelia remove cells into the lumen, whereas cancer-related mutations can redirect extrusion into underlying tissue, a route that could favor invasion. The main visible message is that S1P/S1P2 signaling and cytoskeletal polarity help determine whether extrusion is apical or basal.",
